  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "Password hashing uses MD5, a cryptographically broken and fast hash, making stored password hashes vulnerable to brute-force and rainbow-table attacks. Use a dedicated password hashing algorithm such as bcrypt, scrypt, or Argon2 with a per-user salt.",
      "category": "other",
      "severity": "medium"
    },
    {
      "description": "verify_password compares hashes with the == operator, which is not constant-time and can enable timing-based side-channel attacks. Use a constant-time comparison such as hmac.compare_digest.",
      "category": "logic-bug",
      "severity": "low"
    },
    {
      "description": "No per-user salt is used; identical passwords produce identical hashes, increasing risk of precomputation attacks. Password hashes should be salted before hashing.",
      "category": "other",
      "severity": "medium"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "HOLD",
  "fix_description": "Replace the legacy MD5-based password store with a secure password hashing scheme. Concretely: (1) use a dedicated password-hashing library such as `bcrypt` (or `argon2-cffi`/`scrypt`) and generate a random per-user salt; store the combined algorithm+salt+hash in `password_hash` (e.g. bcrypt format `$2b$...`). (2) Rewrite `hash_password` to call the library's salt-and-hash function. (3) Rewrite `verify_password` to use the library's constant-time verification; if any manual comparison is needed, use `hmac.compare_digest` instead of `==`. (4) Add a migration path for existing MD5 hashes: either rehash on successful login or provide a one-time conversion script. (5) Update tests to cover new hashing and legacy rehash behavior. This is a security-sensitive dependency/behavior change, so it should be treated as needing human approval before execution.",
